<section id="section-1" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
  <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
   <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">1. Before the Visit: 4 Documents OEM Buyers Must Request</h2>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Never board the plane without these four in your inbox. They cost nothing to request and disqualify 20-30% of suppliers before the flight.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Doc 1: Business License with Unified Social Credit Code</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Every registered Chinese business has a Unified Social Credit Code (统一社会信用代码): 18 characters, alphanumeric. Cross-check it on the National Enterprise Credit Information Publicity System. Confirm registered capital, established date, business scope, and legal representative. A factory claiming 5,000 m² and 10 years of operation with registered capital under RMB 1M is almost certainly a trading company.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Doc 2: ISO 9001 Certificate: Verify at the Registrar</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The certificate PDF lists the certification body (BSI, SGS, TÜV Rheinland). Every legitimate registrar has an online lookup. If the certificate does not resolve on the registrar's site, or the address does not match the business license, you are dealing with a fabricated cert. This 5-minute check catches 15% of self-declared "ISO 9001" suppliers.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Doc 3: Baidu Maps Satellite View</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Search the factory address on Baidu Maps and switch to satellite view. Compare the building footprint to the claimed floor area. A factory claiming 5,000 m² that occupies a 400 m² unit in a mixed-use building is misrepresenting scale. This also reveals whether the "factory" address is actually a residential tower or a Shenzhen SEZ industrial park.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Doc 4: Names of QC Manager and Production Manager</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Ask for both, in Chinese and pinyin. Greet them by name on arrival. If the sales representative fumbles the names, or if the QC manager "is on vacation this week", the audit target is likely a virtual factory.</p>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="section-2" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
  <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
   <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">2. Zone 1: The Gate and Reception (First 15 Minutes)</h2>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The first 15 minutes at the gate reveal whether a factory is real or staged. Four signals to check: full company signage matching the business license, a visitor log with 3-10 daily entries, a client photo wall with recognizable brands, and a dedicated (not shared) entrance.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 1: Signage and Company Branding</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Legitimate factories display the full company name in Chinese and English on the gate, with logo consistent with the business license and website. A factory with no visible signage, or with signage that differs from the registered name, is a red flag. Often a shared building where multiple trading companies rotate their "factory tour" clients.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 2: Guard Log</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Ask to see the visitor log at the security desk. A busy OEM factory logs 3-10 visitors per day (auditors, freight forwarders, component vendors). An empty log or a log with only your visit recorded suggests staged operations.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 3: Reception Area</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Look for: trophies from industry associations, a client photo wall with recognizable brands, a display case of production samples, and certification frames (ISO 9001, CE, FCC) with visible dates. Empty walls or generic "office" decor at a claimed manufacturer is diagnostic.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 4: Red Flag: The Shared Entrance</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">If the sales representative takes you through a lobby shared with unrelated businesses (a logistics company, a trading firm, a residential floor), the "factory" is renting a floor in a mixed-use building. Real manufacturers own or lease dedicated industrial floors with restricted access.</p>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="section-3" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
  <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
   <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">3. Zone 2: The SMT Line (30 Minutes)</h2>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Surface Mount Technology (SMT) is where PCB boards are assembled. A real charger factory has one or more SMT lines running daily. This zone catches trading companies faster than any other.</p>

   <img src="/image/factory/factory-smt-line.webp" alt="SMT surface mount pick-and-place production line charger PCBA WOWOHCOOL Shenzhen factory" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="800" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-md mb-6">

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 5: SMT Machine Brand and Vintage</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Look at the brand plate on the pick-and-place machines. Legitimate factories run Panasonic (NPM series), Yamaha (YSM series), JUKI (RS series), or Fuji (NXT). No serious charger OEM runs unbranded machines. A factory showing you "brand-less" or heavily-taped-over equipment is either using counterfeit machines or has borrowed the line for the tour.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 6: Reflow Oven Configuration</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The reflow oven should have dual-side heating (top + bottom zones) and a temperature-profile printout posted next to the machine. Ask what the peak temperature is for the current profile: the answer for lead-free SAC305 solder should be 240-250 °C. A vague answer is a red flag.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 7: Solder Paste Inspection (SPI)</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A real SMT line has an SPI machine after paste printing, before component placement. This is 3D metrology equipment (KOH YOUNG, MIRTEC, VI Technology). Its absence means the line cannot catch paste defects, which produce 30% of charger field failures.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 8: Automated Optical Inspection (AOI)</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">An AOI machine after reflow scans every board for placement and solder defects. On real production lines, AOI runs at 30-60 boards per minute. If the AOI is off, or the operator says "we run it later", the factory is not using it in-line.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 9: Red Flag: Manual Placement and Dust</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Manual component placement on a charger production line is a red flag except for through-hole rework. Dust on the SMT equipment: accumulation on belts, feeders, or reflow oven exterior. Means the line has not run recently. Real production lines are cleaned and warm.</p>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="section-4" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
  <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
   <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">4. Zone 3: The Assembly Line (30 Minutes)</h2>

   <img src="/image/blog/how-to-choose-factory/wireless-charger-factory-quality-inspection.webp" alt="Charger assembly line ESD workstation IPQC inspection China OEM factory audit" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="800" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-md mb-6">

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 10: ESD Protection</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Every worker on an electronics assembly line wears an anti-static wristband tethered to the workstation, and an ESD smock. Floors are ESD-mat covered. A line where workers are in casual clothing with no wristbands cannot produce chargers with a defect rate below 3%.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 11: Parts Bins with Batch Codes</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Component bins should be labeled with the part number, supplier, and batch/lot code. Blank bins or hand-written labels are diagnostic of a factory that cannot trace defects back to specific component lots. Meaning quality problems cannot be isolated.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 12: In-Process QC on the Line</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The In-Process QC (IPQC) inspector should be physically on the line, checking every 20-50 units. If IPQC is described as "in the office" or "at the end of shift", the line is not being monitored during production.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 13: Assembly Speed Reality Check</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Ask the production manager the daily output per line and compare to what you see. A line with 15 workers producing "5,000 units/day" of GaN chargers is technically possible; a line with 5 workers claiming the same throughput is misrepresenting capacity. Math check: at 2 workers per station, 8-hour shift, and 45-second cycle time per unit, one station produces roughly 640 units/day.</p>

   <div class="bg-brandBlue/5 border-l-4 border-brandOrange rounded-r-xl p-6 mt-6">
    <p class="text-[11px] font-black text-brandOrange uppercase tracking-widest mb-2">Expert Insight</p>
    <blockquote class="text-slate-700 text-base italic leading-relaxed">"I had a US importer save $400 by skipping the on-site audit and lose $47,000 six weeks later when the aging step turned out to be photo-only. The aging jig I saw at my last visit? Empty every time. The factory ran aging for the audit and turned it off after. On-site audit is not overhead — it is the cheapest insurance premium in the sourcing stack."</blockquote>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 mt-2">— Nina Nico, Global Procurement & Sourcing Manager, WOWOHCOOL</p>
   </div>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="section-5" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
  <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
   <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">5. Zone 4: The Aging Room (Critical for Chargers)</h2>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The aging room is where finished chargers run under load for hours before packing. This is the highest-value zone in a charger factory and where quality separates from mediocrity.</p>

   <img src="/image/blog/power-bank/power-bank-aging-test.webp" alt="Charger aging room 100% four-hour aging test WOWOHCOOL 45 degree factory QC" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="800" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-md mb-6">

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 14: 100% 4-Hour Aging vs Batch Sampling</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">WOWOHCOOL runs 100% four-hour aging at 45 °C — every charger, every batch. Industry-average is 2-hour batch sampling covering 2-5% of units. Ask the QC manager what percentage of units go through aging and for how long. Answers below "100% for 4 hours" indicate a factory that has not caught up to modern QC.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 15: Aging Jig Capacity</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Count the aging jigs on the wall and the units per jig. Multiply by the four-hour cycle. If the claimed daily throughput exceeds aging capacity, either the aging is not 100% or the daily throughput number is inflated.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 16: The Day's Aging Log</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Request today's aging log. It should show: batch numbers under test, start/end times, ambient temperature, load current, and any failure events. A blank log, or a log with only entries from previous months, means aging is not running today.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 17: Red Flag: Empty Jigs Mid-Day</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">If you visit at 11 AM on a weekday and half the aging jigs are empty with the aging room lights off, aging is running on a "when we feel like it" schedule. This is one of the most common findings in first-time audits of budget suppliers.</p>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="section-6" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
  <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
   <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">6. Zone 5: The QC Lab (Live Test Demonstration)</h2>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The QC lab visit proves whether testing is real: three live tests on production-batch samples, covering Hi-Pot at 3,000 V AC for 1 second, 65W thermal load for 30 minutes (case ≤ 55 °C), and a USB-C PD 3.1 handshake showing PDO negotiation. A serious factory runs all three without objection.</p>

   <img src="/image/blog/car-charger-guide/car-charger-lab-qc-testing.webp" alt="QC lab live Hi-Pot thermal PD protocol testing charger factory OEM audit" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="800" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-md mb-6">

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 18: Hi-Pot Test (Dielectric Withstand)</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Ask the QC engineer to run a Hi-Pot test on a random charger from today's batch. The test applies 3,000 V AC between primary and secondary sides for 1 second — the charger must not conduct. The Hi-Pot machine (Chroma 19053 or Kikusui TOS5300) shows a green pass indicator. A factory that "does Hi-Pot at the end of the week" is not testing.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 19: Thermal Test on Full Load</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Ask for a 65W charger to be loaded to 100% output for 30 minutes and thermally imaged with a FLIR camera. Case temperature should stabilize at 45-65 °C depending on ambient. WOWOHCOOL's GaN V chargers stabilize at 52.4 °C at 25 °C ambient. A charger that reaches 75 °C or above has undersized thermal design.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 20: USB-C PD Protocol Analyzer</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A USB-C PD analyzer (Total Phase Advanced Cable Tester, Ellisys Explorer, or CyberDrive UPB100) shows PD negotiation on a laptop screen. Ask the engineer to trigger a PD 3.1 handshake and show the PDOs (Power Data Objects) advertised. A factory with no PD analyzer cannot verify PD compliance and will ship chargers that fail with new devices.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 21: Ripple + Noise on Oscilloscope</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Connect the charger output to an oscilloscope (Tektronix MDO3024 or Rigol MSO5000 series) with 20 MHz bandwidth. At 15W load, ripple should be below 25 mVpp. Above 50 mVpp indicates poor filtering, which causes device malfunction with sensitive electronics.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 22: Red Flag: Refusal to Run Live Tests</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">If the factory says "our test equipment is being calibrated" or "the QC engineer is off today", the equipment is either non-functional or the personnel do not exist. Legitimate factories have redundant equipment and always have an on-shift QC engineer.</p>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

<section id="section-7" class="mb-16">
 <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
  <div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
   <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">7. Zone 6: The Warehouse and Shipping</h2>

   <img src="/image/blog/power-bank/power-bank-container-loading-export.webp" alt="Charger warehouse bar-coded cartons export loading Shenzhen OEM factory shipment audit" loading="lazy" width="1200" height="800" class="w-full rounded-xl shadow-md mb-6">

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 23: Bar-Coded Cartons with Client PO Numbers</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Finished-goods cartons should be bar-coded and labeled with the client PO number, batch code, and destination country. Unlabeled or generically-labeled cartons ("A1", "B2") indicate a factory that cannot trace shipments. A certain source of mix-ups and reshipments.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 24: Sample Retention Shelf</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">ISO 9001 factories retain one sample per production batch for two years. This is on a dedicated shelf with batch codes visible. If the sample shelf is empty or the retained samples are all from 2023, the factory has not been producing recently.</p>

   <h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue uppercase mb-3">Checkpoint 25: Red Flag: Empty Warehouse or Staging</h3>
   <p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">A "factory" with an empty warehouse is a staging factory: the goods are actually produced elsewhere and shipped through this address for customs purposes. Cross-check the warehouse activity with the guard log entries for freight forwarder pickups over the past two weeks.</p>
  </div>
 </div>
</section>

